#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121134 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the arc length of the curve $C$ given by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= \\left\\langle \\dfrac{t^3}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad    t \\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe length of this curve is given by\n\n$$\nL= \\int_{0}^{1} \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|,$ we take the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ and compute its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩, \\\\ ‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2})^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the curve $C$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}|t|\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this integral using substitution. Let $u = t^2+1,$ then $\\textrm d u = 2 t\\,\\textrm d t.$ Therefore, our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "615541cf-962a-50be-88eb-1d0b64de5509" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7824 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124475 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124287} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049456}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186585050}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124476 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124288} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfd8982d-d859-5eb2-9f35-69af39c04822" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048741}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530438}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Level Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124553 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124362} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62dff73b-1857-5192-ae10-fe22577cbfb4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616967}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125753 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$ with the plane $z=4.$\n\nTo find the intersection, we set both equations equal to each other:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 4\n$$\n\nGraphing the above equation in the $xy$-plane, we get a circle $\\color{black} C$ with center at $(0,0)$ and radius $2.$\n\n![](images/c3/c32f35ccd2c0eb01732194ac419fa328f62f1281c8bf4fb5ccc75218153c46d5.png)\n\nThe curve  $\\color{black} C$ is called a **level curve** of the surface $z = x^2 + y^2.$ The level curves of the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = c\n$$\n\nfor $c=1,2,3,4$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6c/6c86604edc24e92fe51a8eed55386d8b01531b4a4f025e70f7ff33caf0c5df81.png)\n\nThe diagram above is called a **contour plot.** The level curves are sometimes called **contours.**\n\nIn general, a **level curve** $L_{c}(f)$ of a real-valued function of two variables $f(x,y)$ is any set of the form\n\n \n\n$$\nL_{c}(f) = \\left\\{(x,y) \\, : \\,f(x,y)=c\\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a constant.\n\n**Note:** In our example, $x^2 +y^2 \\ge 0$ for all $(x,y),$ which means that the sets $L_{c}(f)$ are nonempty for $c \\ge 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3a86e054-2c30-5b05-8ec7-9293b3a78aa8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2876 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129016} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707430} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707424}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129017} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707297}